# A macro replays every key it recorded, wherever the key went: the
# `/foo⏎` typed into the find bar and the `:s…⏎` typed into the command
# line replay too. A key that fails — a search with no match, `j` on
# the last line — ends the replay (`:help q`); here the search's E486
# ends the `@a` of each `:norm` line once no foo is left.
# Neovim 0.12.5 (`nvim --clean`, feedkeys):
#   qa0/foo<CR>ciwbar<Esc>q @a @@ :2,$norm @a  -> x bar 1 … x bar 5
#   qa:s/x/y/<CR>jq @a on x1 / x2 / x3          -> y1 / y2 / x3
command editor.use_vim
write s.txt "x foo 1\nx foo 2\nx foo 3\nx foo 4\nx foo 5\n"
open s.txt
type qa0/foo
key enter
type ciwbar
key esc
type q
type @a
type @@
type :2,$norm @a
key enter
command file.save
expect file s.txt contains "x bar 1\nx bar 2\nx bar 3\nx bar 4\nx bar 5\n"

write x.txt "x1\nx2\nx3\n"
open x.txt
type qa:s/x/y/
key enter
type jq
type @a
command file.save
expect file x.txt contains "y1\ny2\nx3\n"

